The following parts of lib/engine.js are derived from open-code-review. Every
one of them is MODIFIED: the upstream logic is generalised out of the
code-review domain into domain-neutral primitives that are parameterised by a
domain pack, and the file/function names below are the upstream origins, not a
description of the current code.

  normalizeLine, normalizeExcerpt
      <- internal/diff/resolver.go (normalizeLine)

  matchConsecutive, anchorInDocument
      <- internal/diff/resolver.go (matchConsecutive, ResolveComment)

  resolveAnchor and its three-tier degradation ladder
      <- internal/diff/resolver.go (RelocateAcrossFiles) and
         internal/agent/relocation.go (RE_LOCATION_TASK)
      Deviation: upstream's third tier is an LLM-driven re-location. It is
      deliberately NOT reproduced. This port ends the ladder at "unanchored"
      because an anchor a model guessed is not an anchor an engine can verify.

  DEFAULT_GATE_PREDICATES and their ordering
      <- internal/agent/selection.go (selectFiles, whyExcluded)

  DEFAULT_EXCLUDE_PATTERNS
      <- internal/config/default_exclude_patterns.json

  DEFAULT_SECRET_PATTERNS
      <- internal/config/default_secret_patterns.json

  BUNDLE_DEFAULTS (minFiles, lineThreshold, maxPerBundle)
      <- internal/agent/grouping.go (GROUPING_MIN_FILES,
         GROUPING_BUNDLE_LINE_THRESHOLD, maxFilesPerGroup)

  BUDGET_DEFAULTS
      <- internal/agent/estimate.go (avgMainRoundsPerFile),
         internal/tool/file_read.go (fileReadMaxLines),
         internal/tool/git_grep.go (gitGrepMaxCount)

  critique, runCritiquePanel and the asymmetric loss orientation
      <- internal/prompts/review_filter_task_system.md (the precision-first
         "when your evidence falls short of proof, approve" policy) and the
         protected-subject veto in review_filter_task_user.md

  coverage and report
      <- internal/delegate/* (the coverage self-proof field set)

The following parts of domains/code-review/ are likewise derived from
open-code-review, and likewise MODIFIED.

  anchor.js (normalizeLine, normalizeExcerpt, the sliding-window match and the
  tier ladder)
      <- internal/diff/resolver.go (normalizeLine, matchConsecutive,
         RelocateAcrossFiles). Same deviation as lib/engine.js: the upstream
         third tier re-locates with an LLM call; this port stops at
         "unanchored" and adds two named terminal tiers of its own
         (`locator-mismatch`, `relocation-ambiguous`) so a contradiction and an
         ambiguity are distinguishable rather than both being "no match".

  source.js (hunkHeaderRe and the file/hunk parse)
      <- internal/diff/hunk.go (hunkHeaderRe) and internal/diff/parser.go.
      Deviation: upstream emits one candidate per FILE; this domain emits one
      per (file, hunk), which is the granularity lib/contracts.js §5 declares
      for `code-review`.

  index.js (gate.extensions, and the shape of gate.exclude)
      <- internal/config/allowlist/supported_file_types.json and
         internal/agent/selection.go (whyExcluded). Deviation: this pack ships
         26 extensions where upstream ships 115, so its admitted set is a
         strict subset. It is NOT a full port of the upstream allowlist, and
         COMPARISON.md states the difference instead of implying parity.

  rules/*.md
      Original prose written for this project. It is NOT derived from
      open-code-review's review prompts or rule text, and it is NOT
      expert-validated: every document carries `needs-expert-review: true`.

HOW THIS NOTICE STAYS ACCURATE
------------------------------
This notice is deliberately written so that it cannot go stale, because an
enumeration that must be maintained by hand is the failure mode it would
otherwise introduce: it would be "correct the day it was written" and silently
wrong afterwards.

The rule is a construction rule, not a list:

  * An entry above is required **if and only if** a file contains logic derived
    from open-code-review. The two lists above are therefore the COMPLETE set of
    derived parts.
  * Everything not listed there is original **by construction** — no further
    enumeration is needed, and none is given.
  * A file added later needs no edit to this notice unless it ports upstream
    code. When it does, add the file, its upstream origin, and what was changed
    (every derived part in this product is MODIFIED, never a verbatim copy).
  * `domains/*/anchor.js` carries its OWN `PORTING:` annotation, and this notice
    defers to it rather than enumerating those files. Concretely: every
    domain-owner anchor verifier that reproduces the `normalizeLine` /
    sliding-window primitives registered above for `lib/engine.js` REUSES those
    already-registered entries — the upstream origin is named once, above, and a
    reproduction of it creates no new derived part and needs no new entry here.
    (At present the reuse is a literal reproduction of the expression rather
    than an import; that duplication is recorded as debt in
    docs/domain-contract-v2.md §1.2 "结构债" and does not change what is owed
    upstream.) An `anchor.js` whose annotation reads "nothing in this file is
    ported from open-code-review" is original by that declaration. Therefore a
    newly migrated domain needs no edit to this notice unless its own `PORTING:`
    line names an upstream file that is not already listed above.

    One cross-reference to be precise about, because four `anchor.js` files cite
    it: `domains/code-review/anchor.js` reproduces `normalizeLine`, the
    sliding-window match and the degradation ladder — the same primitives
    registered under `lib/engine.js` above (with the same deviation: the ladder
    ends at "unanchored" and adds `locator-mismatch` / `relocation-ambiguous`).
    The list above is headed "parts of lib/engine.js", so that file has no
    separate entry of its own; those citations mean "the primitive named above",
    which is the reading this rule intends. No part of this product is derived
    from open-code-review other than what is named in the two lists above plus
    the reproductions they cover.

For the record, the following are original and have no upstream counterpart:
the contract kernel and the v2 runtime (lib/contracts.js, lib/domain-loader.js,
lib/reasoner.js), the domain registry (lib/registry.js), the nineteen built-in
packs (lib/domains.js), the on-demand tool activation lifecycle and the service
facade (index.js), the recall-first loss orientation, the anchor-recomputation
policy added in t17 (the engine recomputes every anchor through the domain's
verifier and never trusts a caller's self-report), and the test suites.

That paragraph is informational only. The two lists above are the authoritative
statement: they name everything derived, and the rule above says how to extend
them.
